From: Armada <noreply@armada.local> [Pocknix port]
Subject: [PATCH] input: haptics: serialize the rumble bridge against remove

Pocknix port of the ArmadaOS patch
`1003-input-haptics-fix-rumble-bridge-atomic-sleep.patch`
(armada-os/armada, packages/kernel/patches/).

WHY THE RENUMBERING
-------------------
ArmadaOS numbers its patch set independently of ours and the numbers collide:
armada `1003` is this haptics fix, while OUR `1003` is
`1003-arm64-dts-qcom-ayn-cq8725s-common-hv-haptics.patch`. Filed here as
`2101-`, sorting after the whole `20-sm8750` stack, provenance in the name.

WHAT THE BUG WAS
----------------
The qcom_spmi_haptics_global_* bridge used to wrap the haptics driver's
upload, playback and erase callbacks in the haptics input device's
event_lock. Those callbacks sleep (SPMI regmap I/O, the play mutex,
cancel_work_sync against the set_gain/stop workers), and RSInput's playback
callback additionally runs under its own input device's event_lock with
interrupts disabled -- so the bridge's spin_unlock_irq re-enabled interrupts
inside the outer critical section and underflowed the preempt count. On the
AYN Odin 3 that produced "BUG: scheduling while atomic" on every rumble stop
and intermittent hard lockups.

WHAT WE ALREADY HAD (why this patch is smaller than ArmadaOS's)
--------------------------------------------------------------
Our own `1004-input-qcom-hv-haptics-defer-rsinput-playback.patch` already
fixed the atomic-context half of this, from the haptics side instead of the
rsinput side:

  - `chip->global_ff_lock`, a mutex, serializes upload and gain in process
    context, replacing the `event_lock` spin_lock_irq in those two bridges;
  - `chip->global_playback_work` defers the sleeping
    haptics_playback()/haptics_erase() to a work item, so the atomic caller
    only takes `global_playback_lock` and does schedule_work();
  - that work is drained in `haptics_remove()` and in
    `haptics_suspend_config()`, together with `stop_work`.

ArmadaOS instead defers on the *rsinput* side (a `rumble_work` on
`system_freezable_wq`) and then takes its `haptics_global_lock` mutex in all
three bridge functions, including `..._global_playback()`.

ADAPTATION: what changed, and what was left out
-----------------------------------------------
1. PORTED (the two things ArmadaOS's mutex adds that we did NOT have):
   - `haptics_global_lock` now guards publication of `phapchip` in
     `haptics_probe()` and its teardown in `haptics_remove()`, and is held
     across the pointer dereference in
     `qcom_spmi_haptics_global_upload()` and
     `qcom_spmi_haptics_global_set_gain()`. That closes the use-after-free
     window where a bridge call could load `phapchip` and keep using it while
     remove ran. Both are process-context callers (the input_ff_upload and
     input_ff_set_gain ioctl paths), so the sleeping mutex is safe there.
   - `cancel_work_sync(&chip->set_gain_work)` added next to the existing drains
     in `haptics_suspend_config()`, so a set_gain timer armed before sleep
     cannot fire into the resume path and race the first post-wake rumble.
     ArmadaOS does this; our `1004` only drained `stop_work`.

2. DELIBERATELY NOT PORTED -- taking the mutex in `..._global_playback()`. In
   OUR tree that call still comes straight from RSInput's `ff->playback`,
   which the input core runs under `guard(spinlock_irqsave)(&dev->event_lock)`
   (input.c: input_event() -> input_ff_event(), ff-core.c). Adding a
   `mutex_lock()` there would put a sleeping lock under event_lock with
   interrupts disabled, i.e. it would REINTRODUCE the very
   `scheduling while atomic` this series exists to remove. ArmadaOS can take
   that mutex only because its rsinput half already defers the call to process
   context; ours does not need to, because our `1004` already keeps that path
   non-sleeping. A comment at the call site records this, so the absent mutex
   is not later mistaken for an oversight.

3. DELIBERATELY NOT PORTED -- the whole rsinput half: the `rumble_work` member,
   `rsinput_rumble_work()`, queueing on `system_freezable_wq`,
   `input_set_drvdata(drv->input, drv)`, `INIT_WORK()`, the `flush_work()` in
   `rsinput_remove()`, the new `rsinput_suspend()` and the
   `DEFINE_SIMPLE_DEV_PM_OPS` change. It is redundant here: our `1004` already
   defers the playback to `global_playback_work` and drains it on remove and on
   suspend, and our tree already has `rsinput_suspend()` / `rsinput_pm_ops`
   taking suspend+resume and powering the MCU off across sleep (from
   `0508`/`1007`). A second work item per rumble would only add a queue hop.

PATCH FORMAT
------------
Regenerated against our patched tree (kernel 7.2.6 + the full 20-sm8750 stack,
which includes our 1000-1004 haptics work), so it applies with ZERO offset and
ZERO fuzz. Against the ArmadaOS original, 4 of its 7 hunks in
qcom-hv-haptics.c and 3 of its 8 in rsinput.c failed to apply.

Signed-off-by: Armada <noreply@armada.local>
---diff --git a/drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c b/drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c
--- a/drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c
+++ b/drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c
@@ -866,6 +866,17 @@
 static struct haptics_chip *phapchip;
 static void haptics_global_playback_work(struct work_struct *work);
 
+/*
+ * Guards phapchip against remove and serializes the exported bridge against
+ * a concurrent teardown. The bridged upload and set_gain callbacks sleep, so
+ * their bridge callers must be in process context.
+ *
+ * qcom_spmi_haptics_global_playback() is deliberately NOT serialized with this
+ * mutex: rsinput calls it from its ff->playback callback, which the input core
+ * runs under dev->event_lock with interrupts disabled. See the comment there.
+ */
+static DEFINE_MUTEX(haptics_global_lock);
+
 bool qcom_haptics_vi_sense_is_enabled(void)
 {
 	if (!phapchip)
@@ -6538,7 +6549,9 @@
 //		dev_err(chip->dev, "Creating debugfs failed, rc=%d\n", rc);
 
 	haptics_runtime_autosuspend(chip);
+	mutex_lock(&haptics_global_lock);
 	phapchip = chip;
+	mutex_unlock(&haptics_global_lock);
 	return 0;
 
 remove_v_gain_error:
@@ -6554,7 +6567,9 @@
 {
 	struct haptics_chip *chip = dev_get_drvdata(&pdev->dev);
 
+	mutex_lock(&haptics_global_lock);
 	phapchip = NULL;
+	mutex_unlock(&haptics_global_lock);
 	cancel_work_sync(&chip->global_playback_work);
 	if (chip->pbs_node)
 		of_node_put(chip->pbs_node);
@@ -6614,6 +6629,8 @@
 	int rc;
 
 	cancel_work_sync(&chip->global_playback_work);
+	/* Pending workers must not fire into the resume path */
+	cancel_work_sync(&chip->set_gain_work);
 	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&chip->stop_work);
 
 	mutex_lock(&play->lock);
@@ -6785,15 +6802,21 @@
 
 int qcom_spmi_haptics_global_upload(struct ff_effect *effect)
 {
-	struct haptics_chip *chip = READ_ONCE(phapchip);
+	struct haptics_chip *chip;
 	int ret;
 
-	if (!chip)
+	/* Process context (the input_ff_upload ioctl), so the guard is safe */
+	mutex_lock(&haptics_global_lock);
+	chip = phapchip;
+	if (!chip) {
+		mutex_unlock(&haptics_global_lock);
 		return -ENODEV;
+	}
 
 	mutex_lock(&chip->global_ff_lock);
 	ret = haptics_upload_effect(chip->input_dev, effect, NULL);
 	mutex_unlock(&chip->global_ff_lock);
+	mutex_unlock(&haptics_global_lock);
 
 	return ret;
 }
@@ -6803,6 +6826,16 @@
 	struct haptics_chip *chip = READ_ONCE(phapchip);
 	unsigned long flags;
 
+	/*
+	 * Called from rsinput's ff->playback, i.e. from input_event() below
+	 * guard(spinlock_irqsave)(&dev->event_lock) -> input_ff_event(). This
+	 * path must stay non-sleeping, so it does NOT take haptics_global_lock
+	 * and must not call the sleeping haptics_playback()/haptics_erase()
+	 * directly. It records the latest request under global_playback_lock and
+	 * defers the real work to global_playback_work, which is drained in
+	 * haptics_remove() and in haptics_suspend_config(). That deferral is the
+	 * fix for the BUG: scheduling while atomic on every rumble stop.
+	 */
 	if (!chip)
 		return -ENODEV;
 
@@ -6818,10 +6851,15 @@
 
 int qcom_spmi_haptics_global_set_gain(u16 gain)
 {
-	struct haptics_chip *chip = READ_ONCE(phapchip);
+	struct haptics_chip *chip;
 
-	if (!chip)
+	/* Process context, so the guard is safe here */
+	mutex_lock(&haptics_global_lock);
+	chip = phapchip;
+	if (!chip) {
+		mutex_unlock(&haptics_global_lock);
 		return -ENODEV;
+	}
 
 	//max is 0x7fff, and minimum is 0x4000 the same values android uses
 	gain = clamp(gain, 0x4000, 0x7fff);
@@ -6829,6 +6867,7 @@
 	mutex_lock(&chip->global_ff_lock);
 	haptics_set_gain(chip->input_dev, gain);
 	mutex_unlock(&chip->global_ff_lock);
+	mutex_unlock(&haptics_global_lock);
 
 	return 0;
 }
